# The rules of the coop story (src/game/App/Party.c, include/scrap/party.h; embedded into the game). One rule a line:
# its name, then its words. A word that starts with # ends the line.

# SceneSkip: who ends a scene for every member with Escape (src/game/App/NetScene.c, -mp2).
#   all   every member who sees the scene asked (the default)
#   any   the first member who asks
#   host  player 0 alone
#   none  nobody, the scene plays to its end
SceneSkip all

# The save variables that are a member's own, not the story's (where it goes on, its character, money and alarm,
# its time): they stay out of the party's story. On -mp2 every other variable is the party's, also those a sandbox
# keeps per player (the PLAYER prefixes of tools/script/netclass/savevar.tsv: the hangar, the lives, the bets).
local Map Spawn ComeFrom PrevMap Char AcBatteries Money Alarm AlarmActive AlarmStatus GameplayTime
# The variables of the story the HUD shows: the clients get them and their globals of the same name (old path).
hud EnergyBarActive SpecialActionActive
# The variables of the story the clients' copies leave out: the mission (a client's level start would run it), the
# auto save of a level's start, the instance's level start message (old path).
clientnot Mission.Map Mission.Library AutoSaveGameOnLoad InitialMessage
# The way point of a chapter's travel, given to the start of the target level as single player's start has it.
entry Map Spawn ComeFrom PrevMap

# The mission callbacks of boarding and leaving a ship (Parking.SwitchVehicleMissionCallback and the others):
#   first  single player has one pilot: they run for the first member in a ship, leaving for that member
#   each   for every member
pilot first
# Seconds until a failed mission's level starts again (single player's game over), a super deal's scores stand
# before their Continue, units between the members placed next to the member of a scene after it.
failsecs 5
dealsecs 8
gather 160

# "The player" of the scripts in the world's context of a coop instance: read <site> <rule> [<mission>]. The site is
# the C function that asks (Mode_Usr*, Players_Any, the money and the alarm of the context) or Module.variable for a
# module variable of a player; the rule PARTY (the acting member, else the nearest), MENU (the acting member), DEAD
# (a living member first: "dead" means every member dead), ALL (a setter for every member), NEAREST, ANY or WORLD.
# Without a mission the line is the site's rule in every mission; with one only while the story's Mission.Library is
# that mission. A site without a line has the rule of its row of tools/script/netclass/playerread.tsv.
